{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>The blessing which you shall hear, etc.</b> In every place where Scripture mentions hearing, there follows after it doing and keeping, or service: ״וְהָיָה עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן וְגוֹ׳ וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם וַעֲשִׂיתֶם״; ״וְהָיָה אִם שָׁמוֹעַ וְגוֹ׳ וּלְעָבְדוֹ בְּכָל לְבַבְכֶם״. For hearing contains nothing but the reception of matters in thought alone, that one accepts upon himself to do them, but in any case this is not sufficient — rather, doing in actual practice is also required, as it is stated: ״וְהַקְּלָלָה אִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ וְגוֹ׳ וְסַרְתֶּם מִן הַדֶּרֶךְ״. If so, it is difficult: he should have said, ״The blessing which you shall hear, etc., and you shall walk in the way״ — is hearing alone sufficient without doing?" "And now lift up your eyes and see what we have written above in parashat Lekh Lekha, regarding what is stated: ״וַאֲבָרְכָה מְבָרְכֶיךָ וּמְקַלֶּלְךָ אָאֹר״ — that for this reason He prefaced His blessing to those who bless, because a good thought Scripture raises upon him as though he had done it, but an evil thought the Holy One, blessed be He, does not combine with deed. And according to this, no explanation is needed here: the curse surely does not take effect until after not hearing and actually turning away from the way, but the blessing takes effect immediately after hearing alone, so that the reception of the blessing may serve as help for him to be able to bring his good thought from potentiality into act through the removal of obstacles." "And as for what He said, ״אֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמְעוּ״, and did not say ״אִם תִּשְׁמְעוּ״ — for the honor of Israel He said thus, as though they are not suspect in His eyes that they would not hear and not accept upon themselves to do the commandments of HaShem even in thought; not at all would they rebel (כּוּלֵי הָאי לֹא פָּקַר), and presumably every man of Israel will intend to do the mitzvah, for thought is very light. But in deed He was uncertain concerning them, for perhaps the service would weigh heavy upon them and they would grow lax in its performance. Therefore He said: ״אֶת הַבְּרָכָה אֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמְעוּ״, for HaShem knows for certain that you will hear and accept in your hearts to do them, and then HaShem will send the blessing so that through it all the external obstacles will be removed from you and you will be able to bring the thought into action — for man begins, and HaShem completes for him, as it is written (Psalms 57:3): ״לָאֵל גּוֹמֵר עָלָי״. Therefore it says ״אֶת הַבְּרָכָה״ — every ״אֶת״ comes to include, for the blessing comes to include the deed that can come about through it. But in the curse the language of ״אִם״ is used, on account of ״וְסַרְתֶּם מִן הַדֶּרֶךְ״ in actual practice, which He stated after ״לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ״, for in this He was uncertain whether it would be or not, for it is possible they would grow lax in practice — for ״וְסַרְתֶּם מִן הַדֶּרֶךְ״ is the interpretation of ״לֹא תִּשְׁמְעוּ״: the intent is not hearing in thought alone, but hearing that contains deed." "And some say that for this reason He prefaced the blessing before the deed, for after all the blessings were: ״בָּרוּךְ הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר לֹא יַעֲשֶׂה פֶּסֶל וּמַסֵּכָה״ — and if so, at every moment he stands in the state of לֹא יַעֲשֶׂה, for if you say that perhaps he will make one after some time, then the matter has no end, and when would the blessing take effect? And this accords with the opinion of those who say (Kiddushin 8a): whoever says on condition that so-and-so says — it is deemed as from now. And the word ״אֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמְעוּ״ means: on condition that you shall hear, and it indicates the precedence of the reward to the deed. And so it says (Psalms 105:44–45): ״וַיִּתֵּן לָהֶם אַרְצוֹת גּוֹיִם וַעֲמַל לְאֻמִּים יִירָשׁוּ, בַּעֲבוּר יִשְׁמְרוּ חֻקָּיו״. And nearby the word ״אֲשֶׁר״ will be explained in another way." "<b>The blessing which you shall hear to the commandments of HaShem, etc.</b> One must examine precisely: why did He say in the blessings ״אֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמְעוּ אֶל מִצְוֺת ה׳ אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי מְצַוֶּה אֶתְכֶם הַיּוֹם״ — once He attached the commandments to HaShem, and once to Himself; but in the curses He separated between them with the statement ״וְסַרְתֶּם מִן הַדֶּרֶךְ״. And it appears to me according to what is concluded in the Jerusalem Talmud, tractate Shabbat (chapter 6, halakhah 9): Rabbi Berekhya said: In the future the Holy One, blessed be He, will make a partition for the righteous inside the partition of the ministering angels, and the ministering angels ask them: What has El wrought? — as it is stated (Numbers 23:23): ״כָּעֵת יֵאָמֵר לְיַעֲקֹב וּלְיִשְׂרָאֵל מַה פָּעַל אֵל״. From here it follows that in the future Israel will hear from the mouth of the Gevurah that which He commands — not like this world, where they said ״If we continue to hear the voice of HaShem, we shall die״ (Deuteronomy 5:22) and required an intermediary, but in the future their power will be so great that they themselves will be intermediaries between Him, blessed be He, and the ministering angels. And this is the essential reward of the righteous and the hope of everyone who hopes to gaze upon the pleasantness of HaShem and to visit in His temple (Psalms 27:4). But the punishment of the wicked is not exhausted by this — that they will not merit to gaze upon the pleasantness of HaShem, according to the opinion of the Rambam — rather, they will receive great punishment in actual fact, according to the opinion of the Ramban." "And with this proposal the difficulty is well resolved: are not the blessing and the curse mentioned here the very ones spoken at Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal? And why, in parashat Ki Tavo, were all the curses recalled — ״אָרוּר הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה פֶסֶל״ and so forth — but the blessings were not recalled? Rather, it is certain that the blessing mentioned here has its essence in the World to Come, for there HaShem commanded the blessing, and the verse explicitly spells out the nature of the blessing, saying ״אֶת הַבְּרָכָה״ — this is the blessing ״אֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמְעוּ״ in the future, ״אֶל מִצְוֹת ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״, for in this world I stand between HaShem and you. This is what it says: ״אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי מְצַוֶּה אֶתְכֶם הַיּוֹם״ — in this world; but in the future you will merit to hear on your own, as stated. But regarding the curse, He did not wish to say that there is no curse other than their failure to attain that perfection — for that is not sufficient; rather, an actual curse shall alight upon the heads of the wicked, and they are those accursed ones recalled in parashat Ki Tavo, and that curse shall alight if you do not hearken to accept the commandments of HaShem your Elohim. And so that you not err by interpreting ״אִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ״ as being of the same nature as ״אֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמְעוּ״, He said ״וְסַרְתֶּם מִן הַדֶּרֶךְ״ — for what I said, ״אִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ״, means turning aside from the path in this world, and it is not of the nature of ״אֲשֶׁר תִּשְׁמְעוּ״, which speaks of the reward of the World to Come." "And as for what is said, ״אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי מְצַוֶּה אֶתְכֶם הַיּוֹם״ — since ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״ they heard from the mouth of the Almighty (Makkot 24), therefore He made two divisions and said that the curse shall alight if you do not hearken to the commandments of HaShem your Elohim — that is, ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״, which you heard with your own ears from the mouth of the Almighty. And regarding these, it is obvious that the curse shall alight if you do not hearken to fulfill them; but even if you turn aside from the path that I command you today, and rely upon emptiness, saying that I invented them from my own heart — even so, you will not be exempted from the punishment by this. And He still did not specify what those commandments are that they heard from the mouth of the Almighty. Therefore He said: ״לָלֶכֶת אַחֲרֵי אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים״ and so forth, for this corresponds to ״אָנֹכִי״ and ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ״, and it is attached to the beginning of the verse, which says ״אִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ אֶל מִצְוֹת ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״. And through this, all the doubts with which the commentators were troubled in this parashah will be resolved."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :register {:flags {6 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {4 "The author alternates between attributing the commandments to HaShem (אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי מְצַוֶּה — to Himself) and to HaShem's name (אֶל מִצְוֺת ה׳ — to YHWH); both are rendered literally, preserving the distinction he draws.", 5 "The opening words וּבְהַצָּעָה זוֹ ('and with this proposal') refer to an interpretation introduced before this excerpt, establishing the distinction between this-worldly and otherworldly reward that governs the entire comment.", 6 "The citation (מכות כד) refers to Makkot 24a; the Talmudic reference is rendered with the tractate name as printed in the standard edition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:46.998049578Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}